  "background_and_question": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Thomas J. Miller",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "49d6c51bdb790a02ca75801975fdfe0062e3fb0de4d226b3c5bc8e004e18d705",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-49d6c51bdb79-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Thomas J. Miller, the modern guilty\u2011plea colloquy focuses on clearly articulating the consequences of pleading guilty, which the parties say constitutes an intelligent and knowing waiver of counsel."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Thomas J. Miller",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "49d6c51bdb790a02ca75801975fdfe0062e3fb0de4d226b3c5bc8e004e18d705",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-49d6c51bdb79-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 8,
              "start": 8
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Thomas J. Miller, the plea colloquy in this case was analogous to the Patterson case, not to the Faretta case of proceeding without counsel."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Theresa R. Wilson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "49d6c51bdb790a02ca75801975fdfe0062e3fb0de4d226b3c5bc8e004e18d705",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-49d6c51bdb79-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 28,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Theresa R. Wilson, the Constitution requires that a defendant be advised of the right to have counsel present during a guilty plea, that waiving counsel eliminates an independent opinion, and that the waiver risks overlooking defenses the defendant may not recognize."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "National Legal Aid and Defender Association",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "49d6c51bdb790a02ca75801975fdfe0062e3fb0de4d226b3c5bc8e004e18d705",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-49d6c51bdb79-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 38,
              "start": 38
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to National Legal Aid and Defender Association, the brief filed by the National Legal Aid and Defender Association outlines the various requirements for plea advisement in both the circuits and the states."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "49d6c51bdb790a02ca75801975fdfe0062e3fb0de4d226b3c5bc8e004e18d705",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-49d6c51bdb79-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice, qUESTION: Would a Miranda warning be sufficient to advise the defendant that you have a right to counsel? Period."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "49d6c51bdb790a02ca75801975fdfe0062e3fb0de4d226b3c5bc8e004e18d705",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-49d6c51bdb79-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 7,
              "start": 7
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice, qUESTION: Well, that wasn't the basis. I didn\u2019t understand that to be the basis of our decision here. The Supreme Court of Iowa did, indeed, focus on whether he was advised about how useful counsel could be."
      }
    ],
